{"from": "artifact:gallio-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "person:gallio", "grade": "A", "note": "The letter names '[L. Jun]ius Gallio, my fri[end] an[d procon]sul' - the same man in the same office that Acts 18:12 names; it attests Gallio and his proconsulship, not the Corinth trial itself."}
{"from": "artifact:gallio-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-12-gallio-proconsul", "grade": "A", "note": "The Delphi letter (nine fragments found early 20th century at the Temple of Apollo) calls Gallio Claudius's 'friend and proconsul' and is dated by its own headings - 12th tribunician power, 26th imperial acclamation - to 52 CE. As proconsuls of senatorial provinces served one-year terms, Gallio's Achaia term falls 51-52 CE; with Acts 18:11's eighteen months and the Claudian edict of 18:2 this fixes Paul's Corinthian stay - the anchor date of Pauline chronology. The attestation covers the office, not the trial's date alone."}
{"from": "artifact:corinth-bema", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-12-before-bema", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated bema is the platform in the Corinthian forum on which a judge sat while cases were heard in the plaza below - the venue type and, per BiblePlaces, one of the rare places a biblical event can be tied to; it attests the setting of the hearing, not that Paul was brought to it."}
{"from": "artifact:corinth-bema", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-17-sosthenes-beaten", "grade": "B", "note": "The beating happened 'before the judgment seat'; the excavated bema attests that venue in Roman Corinth, not the beating itself."}
{"from": "artifact:corinth-synagogue-lintel", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-04-synagogue-corinth", "grade": "C", "note": "A lintel reading [SYNA]GOGE EBR[AION] ('Synagogue of the Hebrews'), found on the Lechaion Road, documents a synagogue in Corinth - but it probably dates to the 5th century per the fetched description, so it is context for Paul's synagogue, not proof of it."}
{"from": "site:corinth", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-01-athens-to-corinth", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated city of Corinth (ASCSA excavations since 1896) attests the city Paul came to, not the journey from Athens."}
{"from": "site:cenchreae", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-18-cenchreae-harbour", "grade": "A", "note": "The excavated harbour of Kenchreai - Roman breakwaters, excavated 1962-1969 under Robert Scranton - is the port settlement Acts names; it attests the place, not Paul's shaving or sailing."}
{"from": "site:ephesus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-19-ephesus-city", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated city site is Ephesus itself, a major city of Roman Asia; it attests the place Acts names, not Paul's visit. Regraded A to B by the architect: the claim also states that Ephesus had a synagogue Paul entered, which the site does not attest, so A cannot cover the whole claim."}
{"from": "site:caesarea-maritima", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-22-caesarea-antioch", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated Herodian-Roman port attests the harbour Paul landed at on the way from Ephesus, not the landing or the greeting."}
{"from": "site:antioch-on-the-orontes", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-22-caesarea-antioch", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated capital of Roman Syria attests the city Paul 'went down' to at the journey's end, not the journey."}
{"from": "text:suetonius-claudius-25-4", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-02-claudius-expulsion", "grade": "A", "note": "'Since the Jews constantly made disturbances at the instigation of Chrestus' ... 'he expelled them from Rome' - the same imperial expulsion order. Whether 'Chrestus' is a garbled Christus or a Jewish agitator is disputed (Evans, Meier, Keener and Stern vs Benko, Judge); Suetonius writes topically, so the text gives no year."}
{"from": "text:cassius-dio-60-6-6-7", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-18-02-claudius-expulsion", "note": "Dio 60.6.6-7 says Claudius 'did not drive them out, but ordered them... not to hold meetings' in a context pointing to 41 CE - a ban on assembly, not an expulsion. Slingerland concludes Suetonius Claudius 25.4 is a different event; Riesner says an earlier action later expanded; Feldman holds the expulsion was limited to the most vocal."}
{"from": "text:orosius-histories-7-6-15-16", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-02-claudius-expulsion", "grade": "D", "note": "The 5th-century Christian writer dates the expulsion to Claudius's ninth year (= 49 CE), quoting a Josephus passage that does not survive; Slingerland and Feldman argue the quote never existed, Smallwood and Cappelletti defend Orosius - a reception text with a disputed date, so D."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-81-84-rome-jews", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-02-jews-in-rome", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus reports 4,000 Jews banished from Rome to Sardinia in 19 CE - a large Jewish community in the city, a quarter-century before Claudius's edict; context, not the edict itself."}
{"from": "text:cicero-pro-flacco-28-67", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-02-jews-in-rome", "grade": "C", "note": "Cicero (59 BCE) speaks of gold sent to Jerusalem 'from Italy and from all our provinces' - Jews settled in Italy a century before Claudius; context for the community Aquila and Priscilla were expelled from."}
{"from": "text:philo-embassy-281-283", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-02-jews-in-pontus", "grade": "B", "note": "Philo (c. 41 CE) names Jewish colonies 'the furthermost corners of Pontus' - the same region Aquila came from, at almost the same moment; it attests the communities, not Aquila."}
{"from": "text:talmud-kiddushin-29a", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-03-trade-taught", "grade": "C", "note": "The baraita: a father must 'teach him Torah' and 'to teach him a trade', and 'any father who does not teach his son a trade teaches him banditry' - the craftsman norm behind Paul's tentmaking; redacted c. 500 CE, it may not describe the 50s exactly."}
{"from": "text:philo-embassy-281-283", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-24-alexandrian-learning", "grade": "C", "note": "Philo, Alexandria's own Jewish author (c. 41 CE), numbers Egypt - whose metropolis for the Jews was Alexandria - among the diaspora colonies looking to Jerusalem: the community an Alexandrian like Apollos came from; it shows the community, not his learning."}
{"from": "artifact:aphrodisias-godfearers-stele", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-04-godfearers", "grade": "C", "note": "The stele (3rd-century Caria) uses theosebeis as a category - Gentile sympathizers attached to a Jewish community existed as a class; later than Acts 18 and from another province, so context only."}
{"from": "artifact:miletus-godfearers-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-04-godfearers", "grade": "C", "note": "The Miletus theatre seat inscription reserves a place 'of the Jews who are also (called) God-fearers' - the same mixed Jewish-Gentile synagogue public; later and elsewhere, so context only."}
{"from": "artifact:theodotos-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-08-crispus-ruler", "grade": "C", "note": "The Theodotos inscription (1st-century Jerusalem) names an archisynagogos - the office 'ruler of the synagogue' Crispus held; it attests the office, not Crispus, and from another city."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-16-160-165", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-14-gallio-refused", "grade": "C", "note": "Rome's decrees (Caesar's letters to the governors of the provinces) guaranteed diaspora Jews their ancestral customs - the practice Gallio's hands-off ruling ('words and names and your own law... look to it yourselves') reflects; it attests the policy, not Gallio's decision."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-nazir-6", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-18-18-vow-shaving", "grade": "B", "note": "The Mishnah lists 'the shaving of one's hair' among the nazirite prohibitions - the haircut marks the vow's completion (Numbers 6 is the biblical base); redacted c. 200 CE, it may not describe 50s practice exactly, and Acts does not name the vow Nazirite. Regraded C to B (reviewer 11, the logged Nazirite ruling): Mishnah Nazir, c. 200 CE, postdates Paul's c. 52 vow but reliably documents the standing custom of shaving at the vow's completion; for a custom claim the gap is not disqualifying."}
